DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       These  functions  encrypt  and  decrypt  64‐bit messages.  The sseettkkeeyy()
       function sets the key used by eennccrryypptt().  The _k_e_y argument used here is
       an array of 64 bytes, each of which has numerical value 1  or  0.   The
       bytes  key[n]  where  n=8*i‐1  are  ignored,  so that the effective key
       length is 56 bits.

       The eennccrryypptt() function modifies the passed buffer, encoding  if  _e_d_f_l_a_g
       is  0,  and decoding if 1 is being passed.  Like the _k_e_y argument, also
       _b_l_o_c_k is a bit vector representation of the actual value  that  is  en‐
       coded.  The result is returned in that same vector.

       These two functions are not reentrant, that is, the key data is kept in
       static storage.  The functions sseettkkeeyy__rr() and eennccrryypptt__rr() are the reen‐
       trant versions.  They use the following structure to hold the key data:

           struct crypt_data {
               char keysched[16 * 8];
               char sb0[32768];
               char sb1[32768];
               char sb2[32768];
               char sb3[32768];
               char crypt_3_buf[14];
               char current_salt[2];
               long current_saltbits;
               int  direction;
               int  initialized;
           };

       Before calling sseettkkeeyy__rr() set _d_a_t_a_-_>_i_n_i_t_i_a_l_i_z_e_d to zero.

